\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:1.35}
[Open and closed are not complementary notions]
Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item There exist subsets of $X$ that are both open and closed (e.g., $X$ itself and $\emptyset$).
\item There may exist subsets of $X$ that are neither open nor closed.
\item A subset $E\subseteq X$ is open if and only if its complement $X\setminus E$ is closed.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, knowing that a set is not open does not imply that it is closed, and vice versa.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:1.36}
[Heine-Borel fails for discrete metric on $\mathbb{Z}$]
Let $\mathbb{Z}$ be equipped with the discrete metric $d_{\mathrm{disc}}$. Then $\mathbb{Z}$ is closed and bounded in $(\mathbb{Z},d_{\mathrm{disc}})$, but $\mathbb{Z}$ is not compact.
\end{proposition}

proof:
\begin{proof}
\label{prf:prop:1.36}
$\mathbb{Z}$ is closed: In any metric space, the whole space is closed (it contains all its boundary points, of which there are none).

$\mathbb{Z}$ is bounded: For any $z\in\mathbb{Z}$, the ball $B(z,2)=\mathbb{Z}$ contains all of $\mathbb{Z}$, since $d_{\mathrm{disc}}(z,w)\le 1<2$ for all $w\in\mathbb{Z}$.

$\mathbb{Z}$ is not compact: Consider the sequence $(n)_{n=1}^\infty=(1,2,3,4,\ldots)$ in $\mathbb{Z}$. For any subsequence $(n_j)_{j=1}^\infty$, if it converged in the discrete metric, it would need to be eventually constant. But $(n_j)$ is strictly increasing, so it is never eventually constant. Hence no subsequence converges, so $\mathbb{Z}$ is not compact.
\end{proof}

