\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:1.13}
[Equivalence of sequential compactness and open cover compactness]
Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space. The following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item (Sequential compactness) Every sequence in $X$ has a convergent subsequence.
\item (Open cover compactness) Every open cover of $X$ has a finite subcover.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

proof:
\begin{proof}
\label{prf:thm:1.13}
$(1)\Rightarrow(2)$: This is Theorem 1.8.

$(2)\Rightarrow(1)$: Suppose every open cover has a finite subcover. Let $(x^{(n)})_{n=1}^\infty$ be a sequence in $X$. If this sequence has no limit point, then for every $x\in X$ there exists $\varepsilon_x>0$ such that $B(x,\varepsilon_x)$ contains only finitely many terms of the sequence.

The collection $\{B(x,\varepsilon_x):x\in X\}$ is an open cover of $X$. By hypothesis, there exists a finite subcover $\{B(x_1,\varepsilon_{x_1}),\ldots,B(x_k,\varepsilon_{x_k})\}$.

But then $X=\bigcup_{i=1}^k B(x_i,\varepsilon_{x_i})$ contains only finitely many terms of the sequence, a contradiction since the sequence has infinitely many terms.

Hence the sequence has a limit point $L$, and by Proposition 1.4.5, there is a subsequence converging to $L$.
\end{proof}

