\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:1.37}
[Existence of completion]
Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space. There exists a complete metric space $(\overline{X},\overline{d})$ and an isometric embedding $\iota:X\to\overline{X}$ such that $\iota(X)$ is dense in $\overline{X}$.
\end{proposition}

proof:
\begin{proof}
\label{prf:prop:1.37}
Define $\overline{X}$ as the set of equivalence classes of Cauchy sequences in $X$, where two Cauchy sequences $(x_n)$ and $(y_n)$ are equivalent if $\lim_{n\to\infty}d(x_n,y_n)=0$.

Define $\overline{d}([(x_n)],[(y_n)]):=\lim_{n\to\infty}d(x_n,y_n)$. This limit exists because $(d(x_n,y_n))$ is a Cauchy sequence in $\mathbb{R}$.

One verifies that $\overline{d}$ is well-defined and is a metric on $\overline{X}$.

The map $\iota:X\to\overline{X}$ sending $x$ to the class of the constant sequence $(x,x,x,\ldots)$ is an isometric embedding.

Every Cauchy sequence $(x_n)$ in $X$ converges to its own equivalence class $[(x_n)]$ in $\overline{X}$, so $(\overline{X},\overline{d})$ is complete.

For any $[(x_n)]\in\overline{X}$, the sequence $(\iota(x_n))$ converges to $[(x_n)]$, so $\iota(X)$ is dense in $\overline{X}$.
\end{proof}
